ow does that  intersect with health well it's a big  movement now on health its value is what  you get for what you pay and whether it  be a drug whether it be a procedure if  it works you should pay more than if it  doesn't work and so developing a system  where we incentivize people for value  rather than incentivize people to do  procedures right now you get sick the  doc gets paid for doing something the  future it's going to be its base  on the outcome and I think that's an  important move the key of Davos that we  all talked about was how do we define  value one of the issues addressed in the  firm's global risk report was inequality  especially when it comes to things like  human enhancement drugs and  bioengineering to quote a section of  that write-up if the price tag is  significant and the benefits are strong  the result would be even deeper and more  entrenched inequality no question you  know we're all talking about electronic  autonomous cars but there may be burning  cars one day if we don't address this  issue we can cure diseases like  hepatitis C we're going to have drugs in  the near future that may make you  smarter stronger and if only the rich  can afford them it's going to increase  this divide that we have now we have to  figure out a way to make you know equity  in terms of dealing with healthcare with  the progress that's happening it's a  major issue today it's going to be a  more of a major issue tomorrow  that's a dystopian future right there of  course speaking of which antibiotic  antibiotic resistance is another issue  that was talked about what can you tell  us about the latest on that you know we  see in this flu season right we have  antivirals so in the first 48 hours you  take one of these a ferret severity goes  way down we're seeing some resistance  this year but there's going to be more  and as we take more and more antivirals  antibiotics drug resistance is a major  problem so we have to figure out ways to  use them appropria